From: Louis Maliyam <louis@nexthop.ai>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] mcu-runtime: sdram_ast2700: cap the detected size

sdramc_size_detect() writes one word at the top of each candidate size
and reads offset 0 once to see whether it wrapped. On Nexthop's AST2720-A2
BMC card (4 GiB DDR5 x16) the probe misses the wrap on cold boot
and reports 8 GiB; Linux then crashes once it uses memory above 4 GiB.
Clamp the result to the board's max-dram-size-mb when the DT sets it.

Signed-off-by: Louis Maliyam <louis@nexthop.ai>
---
diff --git a/apps/mcu-runtime/soc/ast2700/sdram_ast2700.c b/apps/mcu-runtime/soc/ast2700/sdram_ast2700.c
index fb9a1bc..b43781d 100644
--- a/apps/mcu-runtime/soc/ast2700/sdram_ast2700.c
+++ b/apps/mcu-runtime/soc/ast2700/sdram_ast2700.c
@@ -944,6 +944,19 @@ static int sdramc_size_detect(struct sdramc *sdramc)
 	}
 
 	sz--;
+
+#if DT_NODE_HAS_PROP(SDRAMMC_NODE, max_dram_size_mb)
+	/*
+	 * The wrap-around probe can over-report the capacity (e.g. a 4GB DDR5
+	 * x16 part detected as 8GB). Clamp to the board-specified maximum.
+	 * ram_size_ary[] is in 16-byte units, so >> 16 gives MiB.
+	 */
+	while (sz > SDRAM_SIZE_256MB &&
+	       (ram_size_ary[sz] >> 16) > DT_PROP(SDRAMMC_NODE, max_dram_size_mb)) {
+		sz--;
+	}
+#endif
+
 	sdramc->sz = sz;
 
 	/* re-configure ram size to dramc. */
